{
  "description": "The deterministic rules-only referee (SPEC §10.3, §17.4, §17.5; ARCH §12.1; AC-165, AC-167). Single source of truth for web/src/core/referee.test.ts (Vitest) and the iOS XCTest port (SPEC §10.7, AC-168). Each case has an `fn` naming the function under test, a human `name`, its inputs and `expect`.",
  "conventions": [
    "threshold is RC fuzzy_threshold (0.8) unless a case gives its own `threshold`. Normalisation, folds, token similarity and the word lists are the SCR-06 matcher's (shared/testvectors/fuzzy.json conventions; shared/prompts/enums.v1.json).",
    "Claims are scored on every reading the matcher builds for a text (fuzzy.json conventions, D-77): the joined and unjoined spaced-letter sequences, each word with a possessive 's removed (\"Max's\" -> max), and format characters read as spaces (\"Lucas<U+200B>school\" -> lucas school). Every rule is tried on every reading; the strongest result wins (on a tie: the label before the aliases, then the earlier reading) (F-121).",
    "Rule 1(a) never fires for a one-token generic label or alias (\"Hospital\"): generic strings are not terms (D-79, D-101). Rule 1(b): a pair of label tokens counts only if it holds at least one non-generic token, unless EVERY label token is generic (\"City General\" vs \"City General Hospital\" stays exact) (D-101). GENERIC_WORDS includes the common function words a, an, with, from, near, by, to, behind, next, this, that, its, my, our, your, their, his, her (D-102).",
    "`vault` entries are referee vault items {id, kind, label, aliases}. They never carry coordinates: the rules referee never reads them (bands are computed separately, SPEC §10.1).",
    "fn strength: rulesStrength({id: 'x', clueIds: [], ...claim}, vault[key], threshold): the claim gets that default id and clueIds. expect.strength is exact | likely | possible | null; expect.rule names the first SPEC §10.3 rule that holds: exact_a (whole label or alias; for plate / house_number / phone, the SCR-06 digit match), exact_b (>= 2 consecutive label tokens, not two function words), likely_a (one distinctive label token), likely_b (acronym of >= 3 letters, function words skipped, equal to a claim token), likely_c (shared synonym class + an artefact term in the claim), possible_a (shared synonym class, no artefact term), possible_b (D-94: compatible kind, claim confidence high AND at least one non-generic label/alias token matched by a claim token under the matcher's token rule: similarity >= threshold, tokens of <= 3 characters equal, GENERIC_WORDS and their folds never count; never for plate / house_number / phone, which need the digit match). Plate / house_number / phone use the SCR-06 digit match in IMAGE mode (a standalone 1-2 character value such as house number \"14\" counts), because claims never leave the device (F-124). Label and aliases are each tried; the strongest wins (label first on a tie). Kinds must be compatible (§17.4), else null.",
    "An item whose label is empty or generic (GENERIC_PIN_LABELS; the vault store's isGenericVaultString test) is not a referee item at all, its aliases included: a pin (D-36, AC-167), or a text item that validation would refuse (F-124). An empty or generic alias is skipped on its own.",
    "fn vaultItems: refereeVaultItems(items) -> expect, the referee VAULT: {id, kind, label, aliases} only (never a pin's coordinates), generic-label items dropped, generic aliases dropped, input order kept.",
    "Synonym-class words and artefact terms are compared as whole tokens after normalisation (no similarity), so 'badges' is not 'badge'. Label tokens are compared with the matcher's token similarity (>= threshold; tokens of <= 3 characters must be equal).",
    "fn referee: rulesReferee(claims, [vault keys]) -> expect.matches, in claim order then vault order, one per (claim, vault item) pair. reason is the ARCH §12.1 template of the winning rule (local only); route is RULES_ONLY; clueIds are the claim's.",
    "fn merge: mergeMatches(device, rules) -> expect {matches, notes}. Union per (claim, vault) pair, keeping the stronger strength (device wins a tie). When the device found the pair, route is the device match's own route (ON_DEVICE, or IN_CLOUD for an S4 Judge Mode run), even if the rules strength is kept; else RULES_ONLY; reason and clueIds come from the side whose strength is kept. notes is ['+rules'] when at least one valid rules match entered the union, else []. A match whose strength is not exact | likely | possible is dropped. Output order: device pairs first (first occurrence), then rules-only pairs. D-157: a device match for a pair that NO valid rules match links is capped at 'possible' AFTER the union (generic-word false positives from Nano/FM); notes then end with 'device-only: capped at possible'.",
    "All values are synthetic: no real people, homes, schools or plates (CLAUDE.md invariant 9)."
  ],
